WEBVTT

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:02.448
Commas and FANBOYS.

00:00:02.448 --> 00:00:06.248
A comma plus a FANBOYS word joins two sentences.

00:00:06.248 --> 00:00:08.079
A comma alone never does.

00:00:09.611 --> 00:00:11.286
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:15.661
The choices move commas around words like and, but, and so.

00:00:15.661 --> 00:00:18.097
Or around words like however and therefore.

00:00:19.126 --> 00:00:22.001
It's testing how to join two complete sentences.

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:24.323
Here's the method.

00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:28.073
Split the sentence at the joining word, and look at both sides.

00:00:28.073 --> 00:00:32.198
If there's a new subject after it, both sides are full sentences.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.848
Put a comma before the FANBOYS word.

00:00:34.848 --> 00:00:38.278
If it's the same subject doing two things, no comma.

00:00:38.278 --> 00:00:42.153
And if the word is however, or therefore, it isn't FANBOYS.

00:00:42.153 --> 00:00:45.041
Use a semicolon before it, and a comma after.

00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:48.361
Rule one: learn the FANBOYS.

00:00:48.361 --> 00:00:51.276
For, and, nor, but, or, yet, so.

00:00:51.276 --> 00:00:54.441
Only these seven can join two sentences with a comma.

00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:59.406
Rule two: one subject with two verbs gets no comma.

00:00:59.406 --> 00:01:02.608
There's no new subject after and, so there's no comma.

00:01:03.673 --> 00:01:07.073
Rule three: however is not a FANBOYS word.

00:01:07.073 --> 00:01:09.948
Commas alone around it make a comma splice.

00:01:09.948 --> 00:01:13.185
Use a semicolon before however, and a comma after.

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:15.396
Let's try one.

00:01:15.396 --> 00:01:17.646
The left side is a full sentence.

00:01:17.646 --> 00:01:21.421
The right side is another full sentence, so they need a real break.

00:01:21.421 --> 00:01:26.031
However isn't a FANBOYS word, so commas alone can't join these.

00:01:26.031 --> 00:01:27.256
That's a splice.

00:01:27.256 --> 00:01:30.006
And no punctuation at all is a run-on.

00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:32.721
Semicolon before however, comma after.

00:01:32.721 --> 00:01:34.046
The answer is B.

00:01:35.106 --> 00:01:37.356
Watch out for a comma before and.

00:01:37.356 --> 00:01:40.681
If there's no new subject after it, there's no comma.

00:01:40.681 --> 00:01:43.506
The team practiced daily, and won the title.

00:01:43.506 --> 00:01:45.576
One subject, two verbs.

00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:46.483
No comma.

00:01:47.511 --> 00:01:49.381
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:50.441
How to spot it.

00:01:50.441 --> 00:01:51.791
The four steps.

00:01:51.791 --> 00:01:52.896
The rules.

00:01:52.896 --> 00:01:53.921
And the trap.

00:01:53.921 --> 00:01:56.679
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
